<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ngawang Namgyal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1594">1594</a>–<a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ngawang Namgyal was a Tibetan lama of the Drukpa school who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> in 1616 after a dispute over his recognition as a reincarnation. There he built fortress-monasteries (dzongs), repelled Tibetan invasions and unified the country under a dual system of religious and civil government. Known by the title Zhabdrung, he is regarded as the founder of the Bhutanese state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal.jpg" alt="Ngawang Namgyal" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shabdrung_rubin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ngawang Namgyal, known colloquially as the Bearded Lama, was born at Ralung Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa Kagyu</a> lineage-holder Mipham Tenpa'i Nyima. Through his father he descended from Tsangpa Gyare, founder of the Drukpa lineage. In his youth he was enthroned as the eighteenth Gyalwang Drukpa and recognised as the reincarnation of the scholar Kunkhyen Pema Karpo.</p><p>His recognition was opposed by Lhatsewa Ngawang Zangpo, a disciple of Pema Karpo, who supported a rival claimant, Gyalwang Pagsam Wangpo, a son of the Chongje ruler. The ruler gained the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-phuntsok-namgyal">Karma Phuntsok Namgyal</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> and a patron of the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> school, who by 1612 controlled Ü and Tsang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a misunderstanding with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> lama Pawo Tsugla Gyatsho, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> ruler demanded compensation and the surrender of Ralung's sacred relics, to be given to the rival incarnation. Faced with arrest, and following visions in which, it is said, the guardian deities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> offered him a home, Ngawang Namgyal left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1616 for western Bhutan and founded Cheri Monastery at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a> valley. In 1629 he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simtokha-dzong">Simtokha Dzong</a> at the entrance to the valley, from which he could control traffic between Paro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trongsa">Trongsa</a>.</p><p>He subdued rivals in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, including the Lhapa, and in the 1630s unified the warring fiefdoms for the first time, while letting the Nyingma and some Sakya communities continue in central and eastern Bhutan. Under the Tsa Yig legal code he established a dual system of government in which a spiritual leader, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a>, presides over religious institutions and an administrative leader, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-desi">Druk Desi</a>, heads secular affairs. A modified form of this system continues. He also sought a Bhutanese cultural identity separate from the Tibetan culture it derived from.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 1651, and power passed to the penlops, the local governors, rather than to a successor. To prevent a dynastic struggle and a return to warlordism they kept his death secret for fifty-four years, issuing orders in his name and explaining that he was on a long silent retreat. His death is marked as a national holiday, Zhabdrung Kuchoe, on the tenth day of the third month of the Bhutanese calendar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ngawang Namgyal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tibetan lama <b>Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal</b> unified the valleys, defeated three Tibetan invasions, built a network of dzongs and promulgated the Tsa Yig code of law; when he died in 1651 his death was kept secret for 54 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ngawang Namgyal</b>, a lama of the Drukpa school, fled Tibet in 1616 after a dispute over his recognition as a reincarnation.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Zhabdrung Bhutan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punakha">Punakha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bhutan">Bhutan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.62,89.87&amp;year=1631" aria-label="Punakha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6909 1151 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ngawang Namgyal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7359 1421h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7359 1421h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=27.62,89.87&amp;year=1631">What was here in 1631? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
